1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,035
Welcome everyone.

2
00:00:02,309 --> 00:00:07,237
Today we'll talk about what happens
when a feature flag changes, not

3
00:00:07,238 --> 00:00:12,390
just the application behavior, but
the data behind an important KPI.

4
00:00:13,006 --> 00:00:16,963
We normally think of feature flags
as a software engineering tool.

5
00:00:17,641 --> 00:00:22,430
They let us turn a feature on or
off, test something with a subset of

6
00:00:22,451 --> 00:00:24,727
users, and roll it back if necessary.

7
00:00:25,359 --> 00:00:27,834
But there is another side to this.

8
00:00:28,240 --> 00:00:34,676
If that feature changes how events
are generated, which users qualify,

9
00:00:34,968 --> 00:00:40,566
or how a transaction is classified,
then our analytics change too.

10
00:00:41,238 --> 00:00:45,187
The goal of this talk is
to show a practical way to

11
00:00:45,190 --> 00:00:47,128
manage that change safely.

12
00:00:47,527 --> 00:00:52,715
We will look into metric versioning,
reconciliation, lineage, dashboard

13
00:00:52,718 --> 00:00:55,991
validation, monitoring, and communication

14
00:00:56,991 --> 00:00:59,819
Let's start with why it matters.

15
00:01:00,209 --> 00:01:04,545
Imagine that a team changes a
checkout process using a feature flag.

16
00:01:05,096 --> 00:01:08,855
From an application perspective,
the change may be small.

17
00:01:09,348 --> 00:01:13,021
But suppose the new checkout
process changes which sessions are

18
00:01:13,024 --> 00:01:15,072
considered eligible for conversion.

19
00:01:15,848 --> 00:01:18,632
Now, the denominator of
the conversion KPI changes.

20
00:01:19,240 --> 00:01:24,817
That can affect dashboards,
scheduled reports, alerts, forecast,

21
00:01:25,059 --> 00:01:26,630
and executive presentations.

22
00:01:27,176 --> 00:01:32,374
The important lesson is that the
small application change can become

23
00:01:32,463 --> 00:01:34,543
a significant analytics change.

24
00:01:36,295 --> 00:01:42,343
The core idea for this talk is
simple: treat analytics logic like

25
00:01:42,410 --> 00:01:44,399
a controlled software deployment.

26
00:01:44,846 --> 00:01:49,906
Instead of replacing an old KPI
calculation immediately, define the

27
00:01:49,906 --> 00:01:55,158
existing version control clearly, create
the new version, and compare the two.

28
00:01:55,935 --> 00:02:00,602
Then validate the difference,
communicate it, and roll the

29
00:02:00,602 --> 00:02:02,642
new definition out deliberately.

30
00:02:03,240 --> 00:02:07,132
This gives the data team
something very important: control

31
00:02:07,163 --> 00:02:09,118
over the meaning of metric.

32
00:02:10,908 --> 00:02:12,146
Here's a simple example.

33
00:02:12,504 --> 00:02:17,152
Suppose our old conversion
rate is completed orders

34
00:02:17,175 --> 00:02:19,059
delivered by eligible sessions.

35
00:02:19,916 --> 00:02:24,392
Now, we introduce a new checkout
experience behind a feature flag.

36
00:02:25,108 --> 00:02:29,976
The new experience changes which
session qualify as eligible.

37
00:02:30,733 --> 00:02:35,206
Even if the number of completed
orders stays exactly the same,

38
00:02:35,771 --> 00:02:37,243
the denominator can change.

39
00:02:37,983 --> 00:02:40,942
That means that the
conversion rate can change.

40
00:02:41,406 --> 00:02:45,224
So when somebody asks,
"Why did our KPI move?"

41
00:02:45,870 --> 00:02:49,022
The answer may not be that the
business performance changed.

42
00:02:49,615 --> 00:02:54,458
The definition of KPI may have been
affected or they may have been changed.

43
00:02:55,458 --> 00:02:57,667
Where can this risk appear?

44
00:02:58,770 --> 00:03:03,265
The obvious place is the dashboard, but
the dashboards are not the only consumer.

45
00:03:04,071 --> 00:03:09,514
The same metric can feed scheduled
reports, alerts, forecasting

46
00:03:09,516 --> 00:03:13,999
model, planning spreadsheets,
API, and executive presentations.

47
00:03:14,935 --> 00:03:18,798
This is why changing a metric
definition requires us to think

48
00:03:18,813 --> 00:03:20,725
beyond the dashboard itself.

49
00:03:21,337 --> 00:03:25,346
We need to understand the downstream
impact before we can make the change

50
00:03:26,346 --> 00:03:31,302
Feature flags are traditionally
associated with application functionality.

51
00:03:31,769 --> 00:03:38,630
For example, we might say, "Turn the new
checkout on for ten percent of users."

52
00:03:39,438 --> 00:03:45,561
But from a data perspective, we
should ask another question: What data

53
00:03:45,608 --> 00:03:48,117
contract changes when the flag changes?

54
00:03:48,850 --> 00:03:55,358
Does the flag change event names,
eligibility, filters, joins,

55
00:03:55,412 --> 00:03:57,442
aggregation, or classification?

56
00:03:58,088 --> 00:04:05,919
If the answer is yes, the da-data stream
team-- the data team needs to know

57
00:04:06,073 --> 00:04:11,309
about the flag before the in- the--
before interpreting the KPI changes.

58
00:04:13,044 --> 00:04:16,557
Before changing a metric, map it.

59
00:04:17,260 --> 00:04:20,904
Write down exactly what KPI means today.

60
00:04:21,565 --> 00:04:26,965
Identify the source table or events,
filters, business rule, aggregation

61
00:04:26,988 --> 00:04:29,609
grain, and downstream consumers.

62
00:04:30,989 --> 00:04:33,469
Also, identify who owns the metric.

63
00:04:34,322 --> 00:04:39,802
This sounds basic, but is extremely
valuable if a metric definition

64
00:04:39,803 --> 00:04:44,373
exists only inside the SQL code
or a dashboard calculation.

65
00:04:44,722 --> 00:04:46,715
It is difficult to go-govern.

66
00:04:47,597 --> 00:04:52,568
A documented definition gives
us a baseline for comparison.

67
00:04:54,350 --> 00:05:00,688
A metric versioning is one of the simplest
controls we can introduce instead of

68
00:05:00,695 --> 00:05:03,005
silently changing conversion rate.

69
00:05:03,460 --> 00:05:07,364
We can temporarily have a
conversion rate version one and

70
00:05:07,365 --> 00:05:08,605
conversion rate version two.

71
00:05:09,118 --> 00:05:14,815
The versions should have an owner
definition, effective date, and

72
00:05:14,817 --> 00:05:16,203
description of business rule.

73
00:05:16,747 --> 00:05:21,734
This does not mean we need
dozens of pe-permanent metrics.

74
00:05:22,162 --> 00:05:26,481
The point is to make transition
explicit and explainable.

75
00:05:27,049 --> 00:05:31,426
When someone looks at the historical
results later, they can understand

76
00:05:31,609 --> 00:05:33,303
which definition was used.

77
00:05:34,851 --> 00:05:39,539
Now we get to the most important
implementation pattern.

78
00:05:39,976 --> 00:05:42,373
Run the old and the new logic in parallel.

79
00:05:42,907 --> 00:05:47,222
Keep the existing calculation, build
the candidate calculation beside it.

80
00:05:47,561 --> 00:05:54,587
Run both over the same reporting period,
where possible, the same population.

81
00:05:55,218 --> 00:05:58,189
Then store enough information
to compare the results.

82
00:05:58,900 --> 00:06:03,480
The dashboard should not be the first
place where we discover that new KPI

83
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:07,370
is different What should we reconcile?

84
00:06:08,414 --> 00:06:14,027
Start with the basics: event counts, row
counts, eligible population, numerator,

85
00:06:14,042 --> 00:06:20,336
denominator, null value, distinct
values, and day or weekly KPI trends.

86
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:22,317
Then go into deeper segments.

87
00:06:22,677 --> 00:06:26,643
A difference in overall KPI may
look small while hiding a large

88
00:06:26,643 --> 00:06:32,524
difference for one consumer group,
product, geography, or platform.

89
00:06:33,033 --> 00:06:38,427
Reconciliation is about explaining
the difference, not simply by deciding

90
00:06:38,441 --> 00:06:40,245
whether two numbers are equal or not.

91
00:06:41,995 --> 00:06:44,231
Here's a simple reconciliation example.

92
00:06:44,283 --> 00:06:48,736
On Monday and Tuesday, the new KPI is
only point two percent percentage points

93
00:06:48,897 --> 00:06:53,944
higher, whereas on Wednesday, it jumps
to one point eight percentage points.

94
00:06:54,600 --> 00:06:57,711
That Wednesday difference
is an investigation signal.

95
00:06:58,032 --> 00:07:01,696
We should not immediately say
that the version two is wrong.

96
00:07:01,723 --> 00:07:04,175
We should ask what changed on Wednesday.

97
00:07:04,764 --> 00:07:06,067
Was there a deployment?

98
00:07:06,798 --> 00:07:08,517
Did the feature flag expand?

99
00:07:09,019 --> 00:07:11,203
Did the eligible population change?

100
00:07:11,803 --> 00:07:13,594
Was there a data quality issue?

101
00:07:14,505 --> 00:07:17,869
The comparison gives us a
place to start investigating.

102
00:07:19,515 --> 00:07:22,438
A useful investigation
follows the data pipeline.

103
00:07:22,722 --> 00:07:27,523
First, compare the source volumes,
then compare eligibility rules, then

104
00:07:27,524 --> 00:07:32,523
compare joins and deduplication, then
compare aggregation grain, then break

105
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:36,312
the result down by useful segments,
and finally, connect the difference

106
00:07:36,425 --> 00:07:38,341
back to the feature flag cohort.

107
00:07:38,907 --> 00:07:43,956
This helps us move from the
KPI change to this specific

108
00:07:43,957 --> 00:07:46,475
business rule caused the change.

109
00:07:48,262 --> 00:07:52,833
Cohort analysis is especially useful
when a feature flag is involved.

110
00:07:53,153 --> 00:07:57,774
If only twenty percent of the users
receive the new experience, compare

111
00:07:58,195 --> 00:08:00,359
the flagged and unflagged group.

112
00:08:00,964 --> 00:08:06,490
Then examine whether the KPI difference
is concentrated in the treatment cohort.

113
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:14,774
We can also segment by geography, product,
cons- customer type, device, or time.

114
00:08:15,135 --> 00:08:18,943
The feature flag itself becomes
useful lineage signal because it

115
00:08:18,955 --> 00:08:25,538
tells us which users experienced which
business logic Data lineage becomes

116
00:08:25,538 --> 00:08:28,194
an operational in this situation.

117
00:08:28,642 --> 00:08:33,025
We can think of the path as a
feature flag, application behavior,

118
00:08:33,085 --> 00:08:37,999
events, pipelines, metric logic,
and finally the dashboard.

119
00:08:39,259 --> 00:08:46,761
If the document that chain-- If we
document that chain, we can answer three

120
00:08:46,761 --> 00:08:52,605
practical questions quickly: what changed,
where did it change, and who is affected?

121
00:08:52,928 --> 00:08:57,416
Lineage is therefore not just an important
documentation for compliance, it helps

122
00:08:57,416 --> 00:09:02,463
us troubleshoot real KPI production
chains After the metric is validated,

123
00:09:02,463 --> 00:09:04,924
we need to still validate the dashboard.

124
00:09:05,255 --> 00:09:09,819
Check the total trend lines, filters,
drill down, labels, definition,

125
00:09:09,833 --> 00:09:11,544
refresh timings, and data freshness.

126
00:09:12,019 --> 00:09:15,140
Also, check the alerts that
depend on these thresholds.

127
00:09:15,528 --> 00:09:19,974
For important dashboards, it can be
useful to compare exported values or

128
00:09:19,976 --> 00:09:22,973
screenshots before and after the change.

129
00:09:23,640 --> 00:09:27,973
The objective is to make sure that
the dashboard is reflecting the

130
00:09:28,006 --> 00:09:34,976
intended metric, not simply the,
SQL query, runs successfully Here's

131
00:09:34,978 --> 00:09:36,901
the rollout pattern I recommend.

132
00:09:37,416 --> 00:09:42,072
First, define the version one and
version two, then run them in parallel

133
00:09:42,357 --> 00:09:47,622
and reconcilia- reconcile the reports,
review the material differences,

134
00:09:47,659 --> 00:09:51,610
communicate the change, release it
to the limited audience, monitor

135
00:09:51,630 --> 00:09:53,890
the results, and expand gradually.

136
00:09:54,565 --> 00:09:55,454
Only retire…

137
00:09:56,711 --> 00:10:04,002
Sorry Only retire version one
after we have enough evidence

138
00:10:04,030 --> 00:10:07,702
that the new definition is
understood and working as expected.

139
00:10:08,243 --> 00:10:13,077
This is exactly a deployment
process for an analytical logic.

140
00:10:14,210 --> 00:10:18,030
We can think of the entire process
like a software development life cycle.

141
00:10:18,428 --> 00:10:23,503
We move from deployment to validation,
then shadow comparison, and then

142
00:10:23,524 --> 00:10:27,479
limited result-- release, monitoring,
and the final full release.

143
00:10:28,011 --> 00:10:30,733
The important addition is that the new…

144
00:10:30,898 --> 00:10:32,954
We define a rollback criteria.

145
00:10:32,968 --> 00:10:37,389
If the new logic produces an
unexpected result, we should know in

146
00:10:37,424 --> 00:10:42,384
advance what conditions would cause
us to pause or reverse the rollout.

147
00:10:42,938 --> 00:10:48,170
We should also retain an audit
trail of what changed and when

148
00:10:49,170 --> 00:10:51,545
Monitoring should continue
after the release.

149
00:10:52,399 --> 00:10:56,848
Track the difference between the old and
the new KPI while both are available.

150
00:10:57,117 --> 00:10:59,195
Monitor source and volume freshness.

151
00:10:59,534 --> 00:11:04,300
Look at unexpected changes in
important segments which watch

152
00:11:04,314 --> 00:11:08,898
the alerts and thresholds, and
define an observation window.

153
00:11:09,554 --> 00:11:13,113
One common mistake is deleting
the old calculation immediately.

154
00:11:13,280 --> 00:11:17,801
Keeping it temporarily gives us
a reference point when questions

155
00:11:17,811 --> 00:11:19,462
appear after the release.

156
00:11:20,780 --> 00:11:23,550
Technical validation is only half the job.

157
00:11:24,085 --> 00:11:25,980
We also need to communicate the changes.

158
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:30,355
Con-consumers should know what changed,
why it changed, and when the new

159
00:11:30,357 --> 00:11:34,531
definition becomes effective, and which
dashboards and reports are affected.

160
00:11:34,587 --> 00:11:39,052
If we know the expected
impact, we should explain it.

161
00:11:39,490 --> 00:11:44,532
Most importantly, give people an
owner or contact for questions.

162
00:11:44,592 --> 00:11:49,468
A technically correct metric can still
create confusion if the customers do not

163
00:11:49,501 --> 00:11:52,531
know that its definition has changed.

164
00:11:53,527 --> 00:11:55,974
Here's an example of a
practical change notice.

165
00:11:56,273 --> 00:12:00,821
We identify the metric, describe
the business rule change, give the

166
00:12:00,822 --> 00:12:05,579
effective date, explain the expected
impact, and identify the owner.

167
00:12:05,980 --> 00:12:10,605
Notice that we have also s-- we
also state the old definition

168
00:12:10,913 --> 00:12:13,025
remains available for comparison.

169
00:12:13,533 --> 00:12:18,068
This kind of short communication can
prevent, prevent a lot of confusion

170
00:12:18,377 --> 00:12:23,865
when executives or analysts see
a new KPI move after a release.

171
00:12:25,455 --> 00:12:26,663
Let's look, look back.

172
00:12:27,171 --> 00:12:29,831
let's look at the
communication failure modes.

173
00:12:30,364 --> 00:12:33,218
First is silently changing a definition.

174
00:12:33,684 --> 00:12:39,647
Another is changing the dashboard before
the validation ma-- validating the metric.

175
00:12:39,976 --> 00:12:43,592
Another is comparing the totals
without checking whether the

176
00:12:43,606 --> 00:12:44,887
populations are different.

177
00:12:45,382 --> 00:12:51,302
If it is also easy to test only
the overall KPI and miss the

178
00:12:51,302 --> 00:12:52,913
major segment-level difference.

179
00:12:53,458 --> 00:12:57,426
Finally, teams sometimes remove
the old metric too quickly.

180
00:12:58,057 --> 00:13:02,297
The common theme is lack
of control transition

181
00:13:03,297 --> 00:13:07,215
Here's a simple checklist that
can be used for future changes.

182
00:13:07,701 --> 00:13:09,335
Is the definition documented?

183
00:13:09,648 --> 00:13:10,713
Is there an owner?

184
00:13:10,870 --> 00:13:13,350
Is there the-- is the metric versioned?

185
00:13:13,928 --> 00:13:15,364
Is the lineage mapped?

186
00:13:15,967 --> 00:13:18,437
Are old and new values available?

187
00:13:19,069 --> 00:13:21,390
Has reconciliation been completed?

188
00:13:22,238 --> 00:13:24,259
Has the dashboard been validated?

189
00:13:25,050 --> 00:13:27,311
Have the customer been notified?

190
00:13:28,311 --> 00:13:32,393
Are rollback criteria defined
and is the monitoring active?

191
00:13:33,012 --> 00:13:37,645
This checklist can be adapted to
almost any analytics environment.

192
00:13:39,106 --> 00:13:41,596
The diagram summarizes the architecture.

193
00:13:42,485 --> 00:13:45,037
the feature flag controls
the application behavior.

194
00:13:46,242 --> 00:13:48,741
The-- That behavior produces the events.

195
00:13:49,079 --> 00:13:52,605
Those events move through
raw and staging data.

196
00:13:52,958 --> 00:13:56,188
Then we calculate version one
and version two of the metric.

197
00:13:56,234 --> 00:13:58,164
Reconciliation layer compares them.

198
00:13:58,477 --> 00:14:03,777
Finally, the certified KPI is consumed
by dashboards, alerts, and reports.

199
00:14:04,262 --> 00:14:10,177
The key design principle is that
comparison happens before we make

200
00:14:10,180 --> 00:14:13,190
the new KPI the official output.

201
00:14:14,774 --> 00:14:19,643
This change changes the
role of the data team.

202
00:14:19,695 --> 00:14:24,226
Analytics engineering is no
longer completely separate

203
00:14:24,234 --> 00:14:25,463
from release management.

204
00:14:26,115 --> 00:14:31,396
Metric definition become governed
asset, data quality checks become

205
00:14:31,400 --> 00:14:35,734
deployment checks, and lineage
becomes operational, and communication

206
00:14:35,736 --> 00:14:37,916
becomes a part of technical rollout.

207
00:14:38,323 --> 00:14:41,794
This is especially important
as organizations increasingly

208
00:14:41,794 --> 00:14:45,905
depend on metrics for operational
and executive dec-decisions.

209
00:14:46,900 --> 00:14:51,528
If you remember only three rules from
the talk, remember this: First, a feature

210
00:14:51,528 --> 00:14:56,320
flag can change analytics even when it
looks like an application-only change.

211
00:14:56,720 --> 00:15:02,666
Second, never replace an important KPI
definition without comparing the versions.

212
00:15:02,914 --> 00:15:08,046
And third, treat metric changes
as controlled deployment with

213
00:15:08,085 --> 00:15:13,088
lineage, validation, communication,
monitoring, and rollback.

214
00:15:13,763 --> 00:15:18,703
These three principles are simple enough
to apply even in small analytics teams.

215
00:15:19,828 --> 00:15:21,367
Let me close with one idea.

216
00:15:22,554 --> 00:15:26,202
Feature flags give software
teams control over behavior.

217
00:15:26,500 --> 00:15:31,746
Analytics teams needs the same
level of control over meaning.

218
00:15:32,122 --> 00:15:36,418
When business logic changes, preserve
the old definition, test the new one,

219
00:15:36,418 --> 00:15:41,143
reconcile the difference, communicate
the impact, and roll it out deliberately.

220
00:15:41,682 --> 00:15:47,516
That approach helps us protect trust
in the numbers while still allowing

221
00:15:47,528 --> 00:15:49,299
the business to evolve quickly.

222
00:15:49,742 --> 00:15:50,187
Thank you.

223
00:15:50,513 --> 00:15:54,604
I'm happy to take this, any
questions that, you guys may have.

